About: display the potential value (canonical type or case tree) of a neutral

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Stream : Type → Type ≔ A ↦ codata [ x .head : A | x .tail : Stream A ]' -e 'def R : Type ≔ sig ( a : Type, b : (a → Type) )' -e 'def pred : N → N ≔ [ zero. ↦ zero. | suc. n ↦ n ]' -e 'axiom ax : N' -e 'echo N' -e 'about N' -e 'about Stream' -e 'about R' -e 'about pred' -e 'about ax' -e 'about (pred (suc. zero.))'
  N
    : Type
  
  data [
  | zero. : N
  | suc. (𝑥 : N) : N ]
    : Type
  
  A ↦ codata [ 𝑥 .head : A | 𝑥 .tail : Stream A ]
    : Type → Type
  
  sig (
    a : Type,
    b : a → Type )
    : Type
  
  𝑥 ↦ match 𝑥 [ suc. n ↦ n | zero. ↦ 0 ]
    : N → N
  
  ax
    : N
  
  0
    : N
  

A neutral that is an applied canonical type is read back and displayed as the corresponding declaration.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def List : Type → Type ≔ A ↦ data [ nil. | cons. (x : A) (xs : List A) ]' -e 'def Stream : Type → Type ≔ A ↦ codata [ x .head : A | x .tail : Stream A ]' -e 'def Vec : Type → N → Type ≔ A ↦ data [ nil. : Vec A zero. | cons. : (n : N) → A → Vec A n → Vec A (suc. n) ]' -e 'about (List N)' -e 'about (Stream N)' -e 'about (Vec N (suc. zero.))'
  data [
  | nil. : List N
  | cons. (x : N) (xs : List N) : List N ]
    : Type
  
  codata [
  | 𝑥 .head : N
  | 𝑥 .tail : Stream N ]
    : Type
  
  data [
  | nil. : Vec N 0
  | cons. (n : N) (𝑥 : N) (𝑦 : Vec N n) : Vec N (suc. n) ] 1
    : Type
  


"about" on a datatype constant itself (a parameter abstraction reaching a datatype) shows the parameters abstracted and the constructors' output types referring to the parameterized family.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Vec : Type → N → Type ≔ A ↦ data [ nil. : Vec A zero. | cons. : (n : N) → A → Vec A n → Vec A (suc. n) ]' -e 'about Vec'
  A ↦
  data [
  | nil. : Vec A 0
  | cons. (n : N) (𝑥 : A) (𝑦 : Vec A n) : Vec A (suc. n) ]
    : Type → N → Type
  


A canonical type read back at a positive dimension shows that dimension as a superscript on its keyword.  The dimension shown is the *evaluation* dimension, the one it was degenerated by, so an undegenerated codatatype displays without a superscript even when it has a positive intrinsic (Gel) dimension.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def R : Type ≔ sig ( a : N )' -e 'def S : Type ≔ codata [ x .head : N ]' -e 'about N⁽ᵉ⁾' -e 'about R⁽ᵉ⁾' -e 'about S⁽ᵉᵉ⁾'
  data⁽ᵉ⁾ [
  | zero. : N⁽ᵉ⁾ 0 0
  | suc. : {𝑥₀ : N} {𝑥₁ : N} (𝑥₂ : N⁽ᵉ⁾ 𝑥₀ 𝑥₁) →⁽ᵉ⁾ N⁽ᵉ⁾ (suc. 𝑥₀) (suc. 𝑥₁) ]
    : Type⁽ᵉ⁾ N N
  
  sig⁽ᵉ⁾ (
    𝑥 .a : N⁽ᵉ⁾ (𝑥.0 .a) (𝑥.1 .a) )
    : Type⁽ᵉ⁾ R R
  
  codata⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ [
  | 𝑥 .head : N⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ (𝑥.02 .head) (𝑥.12 .head) (𝑥.20 .head) (𝑥.21 .head) ]
    : Type⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ S⁽ᵉ⁾ S⁽ᵉ⁾ S⁽ᵉ⁾ S⁽ᵉ⁾
  



These superscripts are display-only: there is no way to write a degenerated canonical type directly, so the parser rejects a superscript on any of the three keywords.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data⁽ᵉ⁾ [ zero. ]'
   ￫ error[E0200]
   ￭ command-line exec string
   1 | def N : Type ≔ data⁽ᵉ⁾ [ zero. ]
     ^ parse error: invalid syntax
  
  [1]

  $ narya -e 'def R : Type ≔ sig⁽ᵉ⁾ ( a : Type )'
   ￫ error[E0200]
   ￭ command-line exec string
   1 | def R : Type ≔ sig⁽ᵉ⁾ ( a : Type )
     ^ parse error: invalid syntax
  
  [1]

  $ narya -e 'def S : Type ≔ codata⁽ᵉ⁾ [ x .head : Type ]'
   ￫ error[E0200]
   ￭ command-line exec string
   1 | def S : Type ≔ codata⁽ᵉ⁾ [ x .head : Type ]
     ^ parse error: invalid syntax
  
  [1]




A higher-dimensional (Gel-like) codatatype is read back at its intrinsic dimension.

  $ narya -dtt -e 'axiom A : Type' -e 'axiom Aʹ : A → Type' -e 'def Gel (A : Type) (Aʹ : A → Type) : Type⁽ᵈ⁾ A ≔ sig ( x .ungel : Aʹ x.0 )' -e 'about (Gel A Aʹ)'
  sig (
    𝑥 .ungel : Aʹ 𝑥.0 )
    : Type⁽ᵈ⁾ A
  



A permuted Gel-like codatatype is not a record type at all, since its fields can't be projected.  But it is the permutation of one, so it is read back by un-permuting it and applying the permutation to the result as a degeneracy.  Here it is uninstantiated, at permutations of two and of three dimensions.

  $ narya -parametric -e 'def Gel (A B : Type) (R : A → B → Type) : Id Type A B ≔ sig ( a .ungel : R a.0 a.1 )' -e 'axiom A : Type' -e 'axiom B : Type' -e 'axiom R : A → B → Type' -e 'about ((Gel A B R)⁽ᵉ¹⁾)' -e 'about ((Gel A B R)⁽ᵉᵉ¹⁾)'
  sym sig⁽ᵉ⁾ ( 𝑥 .ungel : Id R 𝑥.02 𝑥.12 (𝑥.20 .ungel) (𝑥.21 .ungel) )
    : Type⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ (Gel A B R) (Gel A B R) (Id A) (Id B)
  
  sig⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ (
    𝑥 .ungel : R⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ 𝑥.022 𝑥.122 (𝑥.202 .ungel) (𝑥.212 .ungel) (𝑥.220 .ungel)
                 (𝑥.221 .ungel) )⁽²³¹⁾
    : Type⁽ᵉᵉᵉ⁾ (sym (Gel⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id A) (Id B) (Id R)))
        (sym (Gel⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id A) (Id B) (Id R)))
        (sym (Gel⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id A) (Id B) (Id R)))
        (sym (Gel⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id A) (Id B) (Id R))) A⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ B⁽ᵉᵉ⁾
  



An instantiated one is un-instantiated first, permuted as the uninstantiated codatatype it then is, and re-instantiated, like an instantiated datatype.  This is necessary even when it is *fully* instantiated, although such a one could be un-permuted where it stands by a symmetry of its instantiation arguments: the readback of a codatatype absorbs its instantiation arguments into the self variable, so it would then read as a record type of the lower dimension, which is not something the permutation could be applied to.

  $ narya -parametric -e 'def Gel (A B : Type) (R : A → B → Type) : Id Type A B ≔ sig ( a .ungel : R a.0 a.1 )' -e 'axiom A : Type' -e 'axiom B : Type' -e 'axiom R : A → B → Type' -e 'axiom a₀ : A' -e 'axiom a₁ : A' -e 'axiom a₂ : Id A a₀ a₁' -e 'axiom b₀ : B' -e 'axiom b₁ : B' -e 'axiom b₂ : Id B b₀ b₁' -e 'axiom r₀ : R a₀ b₀' -e 'axiom r₁ : R a₁ b₁' -e 'about ((Gel A B R)⁽ᵉ¹⁾ {a₀} {b₀} (r₀,) {a₁} {b₁} (r₁,) a₂ b₂)'
  sym sig⁽ᵉ⁾ ( 𝑥 .ungel : Id R 𝑥.02 𝑥.12 (𝑥.20 .ungel) (𝑥.21 .ungel) ) {a₀}
    {b₀} (_ ≔ r₀) {a₁} {b₁} (_ ≔ r₁) a₂ b₂
    : Type
  


A *partially* instantiated one is read back the same way, and there it is the only option: a permutation mixes its instantiated and uninstantiated dimensions, while an instantiation can only instantiate the last ones, so it has no un-permuted form where it stands.

  $ narya -parametric -e 'def Gel (A B : Type) (R : A → B → Type) : Id Type A B ≔ sig ( a .ungel : R a.0 a.1 )' -e 'axiom A : Type' -e 'axiom B : Type' -e 'axiom R : A → B → Type' -e 'axiom a₀ : A' -e 'axiom a₁ : A' -e 'axiom b₀ : B' -e 'axiom b₁ : B' -e 'axiom r₀ : R a₀ b₀' -e 'axiom r₁ : R a₁ b₁' -e 'about ((Gel A B R)⁽ᵉ¹⁾ {a₀} {b₀} (r₀,) {a₁} {b₁} (r₁,))'
  sym sig⁽ᵉ⁾ ( 𝑥 .ungel : Id R 𝑥.02 𝑥.12 (𝑥.20 .ungel) (𝑥.21 .ungel) ) {a₀}
    {b₀} (_ ≔ r₀) {a₁} {b₁} (_ ≔ r₁)
    : Type⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id A a₀ a₁) (Id B b₀ b₁)
  



Under glued evaluation, a definition that is an ordinary term rather than a case tree evaluates to a neutral whose value *realizes* that term.  Such a neutral has no potential value of its own, but the one it realizes to may, so we look there; thus a degeneracy of such a constant displays the same as the degeneracy of its definition.

  $ narya -parametric -e 'def Gel (A B : Type) (R : A → B → Type) : Id Type A B ≔ sig ( a .ungel : R a.0 a.1 )' -e 'axiom A : Type' -e 'axiom B : Type' -e 'axiom R : A → B → Type' -e 'axiom a₀ : A' -e 'axiom a₁ : A' -e 'axiom b₀ : B' -e 'axiom b₁ : B' -e 'axiom r₀ : R a₀ b₀' -e 'axiom r₁ : R a₁ b₁' -e 'def Z ≔ (Gel A B R)⁽ᵉ¹⁾ {a₀} {b₀} (r₀,) {a₁} {b₁} (r₁,)' -e 'about Z⁽ᵉ⁾'
  sig⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ (
    𝑥 .ungel : R⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ 𝑥.022 𝑥.122 (𝑥.202 .ungel) (𝑥.212 .ungel) (𝑥.220 .ungel)
                 (𝑥.221 .ungel) )⁽²¹³⁾ {a₀} {a₀} {refl a₀} {b₀} {b₀}
    {refl b₀} {(_ ≔ r₀)} {(_ ≔ r₀)} (_ ≔ refl r₀) {a₁} {a₁} {refl a₁} {b₁}
    {b₁} {refl b₁} {(_ ≔ r₁)} {(_ ≔ r₁)} (_ ≔ refl r₁)
    : Type⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ (A⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ (refl a₀) (refl a₁)) (B⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ (refl b₀) (refl b₁))
        (sym (Gel⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id A) (Id B) (Id R)) {a₀} {b₀} (_ ≔ r₀) {a₁} {b₁}
           (_ ≔ r₁))
        (sym (Gel⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id A) (Id B) (Id R)) {a₀} {b₀} (_ ≔ r₀) {a₁} {b₁}
           (_ ≔ r₁))
  



The field-variable record syntax names the fields of the *whole* self-variable only.  A record whose field types project a proper face of a cube self-variable has no such display -- the projection would have to be written as a cube variable, "ungel.20", which is nonsense -- so it falls back to the self-variable syntax.

  $ narya -parametric -e 'def Gel (A B : Type) (R : A → B → Type) : Id Type A B ≔ sig ( a .ungel : R a.0 a.1 )' -e 'axiom A : Type' -e 'axiom B : Type' -e 'axiom R : A → B → Type' -e 'axiom a₀ : A' -e 'axiom a₁ : A' -e 'axiom a₂ : Id A a₀ a₁' -e 'axiom b₀ : B' -e 'axiom b₁ : B' -e 'axiom b₂ : Id B b₀ b₁' -e 'about (refl Gel (Id A) (Id B) (Id R) a₂ b₂)'
  sig⁽ᵉ⁾ (
    𝑥 .ungel : Id R a₂ b₂ (𝑥.20 .ungel) (𝑥.21 .ungel) )
    : Type⁽ᵉ⁾ (Gel A B R a₀ b₀) (Gel A B R a₁ b₁)
  



A degenerate codatatype or record is read back at its full dimension, showing the in-practice (higher-dimensional) field types, including higher-pi field types with their boundary faces.  It is *not* explicitly applied to any instantiation arguments, since those behave like (non-uniform) parameters rather than indices, appearing (through their fields, as instantiation arguments) in the types of the fields.

  $ narya -e 'axiom X : Type' -e 'def Stream : Type → Type ≔ A ↦ codata [ x .head : A | x .tail : Stream A ]' -e 'def R : Type ≔ sig ( a : Type, b : (a → Type) )' -e 'about (refl (Stream X))' -e 'about (refl R)' -e 'axiom x0 : Stream X' -e 'axiom x1 : Stream X' -e 'about (Id (Stream X) x0 x1)'
  codata⁽ᵉ⁾ [
  | 𝑥 .head : Id X (𝑥.0 .head) (𝑥.1 .head)
  | 𝑥 .tail : Stream⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id X) (𝑥.0 .tail) (𝑥.1 .tail) ]
    : Type⁽ᵉ⁾ (Stream X) (Stream X)
  
  sig⁽ᵉ⁾ (
    𝑥 .a : Type⁽ᵉ⁾ (𝑥.0 .a) (𝑥.1 .a),
    𝑥 .b : {𝑥₀ : 𝑥.0 .a} {𝑥₁ : 𝑥.1 .a} (𝑥₂ : 𝑥.2 .a 𝑥₀ 𝑥₁)
           →⁽ᵉ⁾ Type⁽ᵉ⁾ (𝑥.0 .b 𝑥₀) (𝑥.1 .b 𝑥₁) )
    : Type⁽ᵉ⁾ R R
  
  codata⁽ᵉ⁾ [
  | 𝑥 .head : Id X (x0 .head) (x1 .head)
  | 𝑥 .tail : Stream⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id X) (x0 .tail) (x1 .tail) ]
    : Type
  


For a codatatype with a higher field, every instance of that field that a comatch would have to provide is listed: one per partial bijection between the codatatype's dimension and the field's intrinsic dimension.  This includes the projectable instances (.1, .2) as well as the non-projectable "declaration" instance (.e) and intermediate forms, the latter computed in a context degenerated by the remaining dimensions.  In particular, the undegenerate type reproduces the field's declaration.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def √N : Type ≔ codata [ x .root.e : N ]' -e 'about √N' -e 'about (refl √N)' -e 'about (refl (refl √N))'
  codata [
  | 𝑥 .root.e : N ]
    : Type
  
  codata⁽ᵉ⁾ [
  | 𝑥 .root.e : N⁽ᵉ⁾ (𝑥.02 .root) (𝑥.12 .root)
  | 𝑥 .root.1 : N ]
    : Type⁽ᵉ⁾ √N √N
  
  codata⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ [
  | 𝑥 .root.e
    : N⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ (sym 𝑥.022 .root.1) (sym 𝑥.122 .root.1) (sym 𝑥.202 .root.1)
        (sym 𝑥.212 .root.1)
  | 𝑥 .root.1 : N⁽ᵉ⁾ (𝑥.20 .root) (𝑥.21 .root)
  | 𝑥 .root.2 : N⁽ᵉ⁾ (𝑥.02 .root) (𝑥.12 .root) ]
    : Type⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ √N⁽ᵉ⁾ √N⁽ᵉ⁾ √N⁽ᵉ⁾ √N⁽ᵉ⁾
  


A degenerate datatype is read back by evaluating each constructor's function-type in its higher-dimensional environment and instantiating the result at the eta-expanded lower-dimensional versions of the constructor itself.  This shows what the higher-dimensional constructor can actually be applied to and what its output is (so e.g. cons. lands in List⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id X) (cons. x₀ xs₀) (cons. x₁ xs₁)); a nullary constructor likewise displays its instantiated output, since the boundary of a higher-dimensional datatype behaves like indices.  This includes indexed datatypes, whose indices are degenerated as well (e.g. cons. lands in Vec⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id X) (suc. n₂) …).  Instantiation arguments are given as an application afterwards, just like indices are given for indexed datatypes.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'axiom X : Type' -e 'def List : Type → Type ≔ A ↦ data [ nil. | cons. (x : A) (xs : List A) ]' -e 'def Vec : Type → N → Type ≔ A ↦ data [ nil. : Vec A zero. | cons. : (n : N) → A → Vec A n → Vec A (suc. n) ]' -e 'about (refl (List X))' -e 'axiom x0 : List X' -e 'axiom x1 : List X' -e 'about (Id (List X) x0 x1)' -e 'about (refl N)' -e 'about (refl (Vec X zero.))' -e 'axiom v0 : Vec X 0' -e 'axiom v1 : Vec X 0' -e 'about Id (Vec X 0) v0 v1'
  data⁽ᵉ⁾ [
  | nil. : List⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id X) nil. nil.
  | cons.
    : {x₀ : X} {x₁ : X} (x₂ : Id X x₀ x₁) {xs₀ : List X} {xs₁ : List X}
      (xs₂ : List⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id X) xs₀ xs₁)
      →⁽ᵉ⁾ List⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id X) (cons. x₀ xs₀) (cons. x₁ xs₁) ]
    : Type⁽ᵉ⁾ (List X) (List X)
  
  data⁽ᵉ⁾ [
  | nil. : List⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id X) nil. nil.
  | cons.
    : {x₀ : X} {x₁ : X} (x₂ : Id X x₀ x₁) {xs₀ : List X} {xs₁ : List X}
      (xs₂ : List⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id X) xs₀ xs₁)
      →⁽ᵉ⁾ List⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id X) (cons. x₀ xs₀) (cons. x₁ xs₁) ] x0 x1
    : Type
  
  data⁽ᵉ⁾ [
  | zero. : N⁽ᵉ⁾ 0 0
  | suc. : {𝑥₀ : N} {𝑥₁ : N} (𝑥₂ : N⁽ᵉ⁾ 𝑥₀ 𝑥₁) →⁽ᵉ⁾ N⁽ᵉ⁾ (suc. 𝑥₀) (suc. 𝑥₁) ]
    : Type⁽ᵉ⁾ N N
  
  data⁽ᵉ⁾ [
  | nil. : Vec⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id X) {0} {0} (refl 0) nil. nil.
  | cons.
    : {n₀ : N} {n₁ : N} (n₂ : N⁽ᵉ⁾ n₀ n₁) {𝑥₀ : X} {𝑥₁ : X} (𝑥₂ : Id X 𝑥₀ 𝑥₁)
      {𝑦₀ : Vec X n₀} {𝑦₁ : Vec X n₁} (𝑦₂ : Vec⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id X) n₂ 𝑦₀ 𝑦₁)
      →⁽ᵉ⁾ Vec⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id X) {suc. n₀} {suc. n₁} (suc. n₂) (cons. n₀ 𝑥₀ 𝑦₀)
             (cons. n₁ 𝑥₁ 𝑦₁) ] {0} {0} (refl 0)
    : Type⁽ᵉ⁾ (Vec X 0) (Vec X 0)
  
  data⁽ᵉ⁾ [
  | nil. : Vec⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id X) {0} {0} (refl 0) nil. nil.
  | cons.
    : {n₀ : N} {n₁ : N} (n₂ : N⁽ᵉ⁾ n₀ n₁) {𝑥₀ : X} {𝑥₁ : X} (𝑥₂ : Id X 𝑥₀ 𝑥₁)
      {𝑦₀ : Vec X n₀} {𝑦₁ : Vec X n₁} (𝑦₂ : Vec⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id X) n₂ 𝑦₀ 𝑦₁)
      →⁽ᵉ⁾ Vec⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id X) {suc. n₀} {suc. n₁} (suc. n₂) (cons. n₀ 𝑥₀ 𝑦₀)
             (cons. n₁ 𝑥₁ 𝑦₁) ] {0} {0} (refl 0) v0 v1
    : Type
  


Since the boundary types come from the constructor's own environment (rather than by degenerating a single vertex), a degenerate datatype whose endpoints are *different* instances shows each boundary argument at its own correct type (x₀ : X but x₁ : Y, and xs₁ : List Y).

  $ narya -e 'axiom X : Type' -e 'axiom Y : Type' -e 'axiom A2 : Id Type X Y' -e 'def List : Type → Type ≔ A ↦ data [ nil. | cons. (x : A) (xs : List A) ]' -e 'about (refl List {X} {Y} A2)'
  data⁽ᵉ⁾ [
  | nil. : List⁽ᵉ⁾ A2 nil. nil.
  | cons.
    : {x₀ : X} {x₁ : Y} (x₂ : A2 x₀ x₁) {xs₀ : List X} {xs₁ : List Y}
      (xs₂ : List⁽ᵉ⁾ A2 xs₀ xs₁)
      →⁽ᵉ⁾ List⁽ᵉ⁾ A2 (cons. x₀ xs₀) (cons. x₁ xs₁) ]
    : Type⁽ᵉ⁾ (List X) (List Y)
  



In a parametricity configuration with no endpoints (arity 0), a degenerate datatype still displays: an arity-0 cube has no proper faces, so there is no boundary to instantiate at, but there is still the (empty) instantiation, displayed as ".".

  $ narya -arity 0 -parametric -e 'axiom X : Type' -e 'def List : Type → Type ≔ A ↦ data [ nil. | cons. (x : A) (xs : List A) ]' -e 'about (List X)⁽ᵉ⁾'
  data⁽ᵉ⁾ [
  | nil. : List⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id X) .
  | cons. : (x₀ : Id X .) (xs₀ : List⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id X) .) →⁽ᵉ⁾ List⁽ᵉ⁾ (Id X) . ]
    : Type⁽ᵉ⁾ .
  


A datatype defined nested inside a case tree is reached through the tree rather than as a top-level canonical type, so "about" displays the stored case tree.  Each nested datatype's constructor output types are shown faithfully (with the real datatype head) from the stored output term.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def W (n : N) : N → Type ≔ match n [ zero. ↦ data [ w0. : W n zero. ] | suc. m ↦ data [ w1. : W n (suc. m) ] ]' -e 'about W'
  n ↦
  match n [
  | suc. m ↦ data [
    | w1. : W n (suc. m) ]
  | zero. ↦ data [
    | w0. : W n 0 ]]
    : (n : N) → N → Type
  


A neutral whose value is a comatch (a coinductive record without eta) is read back and displayed as that comatch, by projecting each field.  This includes comatches that are only partially applied, and hence are not stored case trees that could be displayed by name, and degenerate (e.g. refl) comatches of a lower-field codatatype, whose fields are read back at the degenerated dimension.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Stream : Type → Type ≔ A ↦ codata [ x .head : A | x .tail : Stream A ]' -e 'def s : Stream N ≔ [ .head ↦ 0 | .tail ↦ s ]' -e 'def const : (A : Type) → A → Stream A ≔ A a ↦ [ .head ↦ a | .tail ↦ const A a ]' -e 'about s' -e 'about (const N 0)' -e 'about (refl s)'
  [ .head ↦ 0 | .tail ↦ s ]
    : Stream N
  
  [ .head ↦ 0 | .tail ↦ const N 0 ]
    : Stream N
  
  [ .head ↦ refl 0 | .tail ↦ refl s ]
    : Stream⁽ᵉ⁾ N⁽ᵉ⁾ s s
  



A comatch against a codatatype with a higher field is read back by reading back the value of each field instance, one per partial bijection between the comatch's dimension and the field's intrinsic dimension, exactly as the codatatype declaration lists them.  This includes a *degenerate* higher comatch such as "refl r", whose instances (.e and .1, as for the type "refl √N" above) are the corresponding degeneracies of the original body.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def √N : Type ≔ codata [ x .root.e : N ]' -e 'def r : √N ≔ [ .root.e ↦ 0 ]' -e 'about r' -e 'about (refl r)' -e 'about (refl (refl r))'
  [ .root.e ↦ 0 ]
    : √N
  
  [ .root.e ↦ refl 0 | .root.1 ↦ 0 ]
    : √N⁽ᵉ⁾ r r
  
  [ .root.e ↦ 0⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ | .root.1 ↦ refl 0 | .root.2 ↦ refl 0 ]
    : √N⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ (refl r) (refl r) (refl r) (refl r)
  



A comatch that is only partially applied, and hence has a non-empty closure environment, is read back the same way: the environment is transported into the degenerated context, so the body's variables are replaced by their values there.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def √N : Type ≔ codata [ x .root.e : N ]' -e 'def cst (a : N) : √N ≔ [ .root.e ↦ a.0 ]' -e 'about (cst 1)' -e 'about (refl (cst 1))'
  [ .root.e ↦ 1 ]
    : √N
  
  [ .root.e ↦ refl 1 | .root.1 ↦ 1 ]
    : √N⁽ᵉ⁾ (cst 1) (cst 1)
  



Each instance is read back in the context degenerated by its own remaining dimensions -- the same context the instance was checked in -- so inside a hole the body refers to the degenerated forms of the hole's variables (y.0 rather than y), and is a term that would actually typecheck there.

  $ narya -fake-interact 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ] def √N : Type ≔ codata [ x .root.e : N ] def cst (a : N) : √N ≔ [ .root.e ↦ a.0 ] def foo (y : N) : N ≔ ? about in 0 (cst y) about in 0 (refl (cst y))'
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant N defined
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant √N defined
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant cst defined
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant foo defined, containing 1 hole
  
   ￫ info[I3003]
   ￮ hole ?0:
     
     y : N
     ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     N
  
   ￫ info[I3003]
   ￮ hole ?0:
     
     y : N
     ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     N
  
  [ .root.e ↦ y.0 ]
    : √N
  
   ￫ info[I3003]
   ￮ hole ?0:
     
     y : N
     ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     N
  
  [ .root.e ↦ refl y.0 | .root.1 ↦ y ]
    : √N⁽ᵉ⁾ (cst y) (cst y)
  



A comatch can mix lower and higher fields; all are read back, degenerate or not.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def C : Type ≔ codata [ x .head : N | x .root.e : N ]' -e 'def c : C ≔ [ .head ↦ 0 | .root.e ↦ suc. 0 ]' -e 'about c' -e 'about (refl c)'
  [ .head ↦ 0 | .root.e ↦ 1 ]
    : C
  
  [ .head ↦ refl 0 | .root.e ↦ refl 1 | .root.1 ↦ 1 ]
    : C⁽ᵉ⁾ c c
  



A struct at a codatatype with a nonzero intrinsic (Gel-like) dimension is read back too, rather than showing its application spine.

  $ narya -dtt -e 'axiom A : Type' -e 'axiom Aʹ : A → Type' -e 'def Gel (A : Type) (Aʹ : A → Type) : Type⁽ᵈ⁾ A ≔ sig ( x .ungel : Aʹ x.0 )' -e 'axiom a : A' -e 'axiom aʹ : Aʹ a' -e 'def h (x : A) (xʹ : Aʹ x) : Gel A Aʹ x ≔ (xʹ,)' -e 'about (h a aʹ)'
  (_ ≔ aʹ)
    : Gel A Aʹ a
  


In a parametricity configuration with no endpoints (arity 0), a degenerate higher comatch still lists the same instances as the corresponding degenerate codatatype.

  $ narya -arity 0 -parametric -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def √N : Type ≔ codata [ x .root.e : N ]' -e 'def r : √N ≔ [ .root.e ↦ 0 ]' -e 'about (√N⁽ᵉ⁾)' -e 'about (r⁽ᵉ⁾)'
  codata⁽ᵉ⁾ [
  | 𝑥 .root.e : N⁽ᵉ⁾ .
  | 𝑥 .root.1 : N ]
    : Type⁽ᵉ⁾ .
  
  [ .root.e ↦ refl 0 | .root.1 ↦ 0 ]
    : √N⁽ᵉ⁾ .
  


A comatch with an instance that is a genuinely stuck case tree, such as an unfilled hole, retains no trace of what it is stuck on once evaluated, so it isn't read back; the stored case tree is displayed instead, which does still name the hole.  A stuck instance of a comatch that has been applied, and so has no stored tree to fall back on, shows the application spine.

  $ narya -fake-interact 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ] def √N : Type ≔ codata [ x .root.e : N ] def r : √N ≔ [ .root.e ↦ ? ] about r def cst (a : N) : √N ≔ [ .root.e ↦ ? ] about (cst 1)'
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant N defined
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant √N defined
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant r defined, containing 1 hole
  
   ￫ info[I3003]
   ￮ hole ?0:
     
     ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     N
  
  [ .root.e ↦ ?0 ]
    : √N
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant cst defined, containing 1 hole
  
   ￫ info[I3003]
   ￮ hole ?1:
     
     a.0 : N
     a.1 : N
     a.2 : N⁽ᵉ⁾ a.0 a.1
     ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     N
  
  [ .root.e ↦ ap cst {1} {1} (refl 1) .root ]
    : √N
  


The 'deg' of a comatch value is nontrivial when an already-evaluated value is acted on later by a degeneracy, as when it is looked up from an environment with a nontrivial degeneracy on the result.  Factoring an instance's partial bijection through that degeneracy can turn a shared dimension into a remaining one, so the instance's own remaining dimensions are fewer than those of the stored term it is built from; the extra ones are genuinely degenerate and are filled in as such, rather than the instance being dropped.

  $ narya -fake-interact 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ] def √N : Type ≔ codata [ x .root.e : N ] def cst (a : N) : √N ≔ [ .root.e ↦ a.0 ] def f : √N ≔ let x ≔ cst 1 in ? about in 0 refl x'
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant N defined
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant √N defined
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant cst defined
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant f defined, containing 1 hole
  
   ￫ info[I3003]
   ￮ hole ?0:
     
     x ≔ cst 1 : √N
     ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     √N
  
   ￫ info[I3003]
   ￮ hole ?0:
     
     x ≔ cst 1 : √N
     ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     √N
  
  [ .root.e ↦ refl 1 | .root.1 ↦ 1 ]
    : √N⁽ᵉ⁾ (cst 1) (cst 1)
  


With a field of intrinsic dimension two, this happens even for an instance that still has remaining dimensions of its own (.root.e1 and .root.1e have one each, while the stored term has two).

  $ narya -fake-interact 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ] def R2 : Type ≔ codata [ x .root.ee : N ] def cst2 (a : N) : R2 ≔ [ .root.ee ↦ a.00 ] def f : R2 ≔ let x ≔ cst2 1 in ? about in 0 refl x'
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant N defined
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant R2 defined
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant cst2 defined
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant f defined, containing 1 hole
  
   ￫ info[I3003]
   ￮ hole ?0:
     
     x ≔ cst2 1 : R2
     ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     R2
  
   ￫ info[I3003]
   ￮ hole ?0:
     
     x ≔ cst2 1 : R2
     ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     R2
  
  [ .root.ee ↦ refl 1 | .root.e1 ↦ 1 | .root.1e ↦ 1 ]
    : R2⁽ᵉ⁾ (cst2 1) (cst2 1)
  


A neutral whose case tree is stuck on a match displays as that match, with the discriminee and the branch bodies read back, rather than as its application spine.  The pattern variables are named as the user named them in the branch.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def pred : N → N ≔ [ zero. ↦ zero. | suc. n ↦ n ]' -e 'axiom ax : N' -e 'about (pred ax)' -e 'echo (pred ax)'
  match ax [ suc. n ↦ n | zero. ↦ 0 ]
    : N
  
  pred ax
    : N
  

Branches are read back at their own types, so a branch body that is itself a lambda or another match is displayed in full.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'axiom ax : N' -e 'def f : N → N → N ≔ [ zero. ↦ [ zero. ↦ zero. | suc. m ↦ m ] | suc. n ↦ [ zero. ↦ n | suc. m ↦ suc. m ] ]' -e 'about (f ax)'
  match ax [
  | suc. n ↦ 𝑥 ↦ match 𝑥 [ suc. m ↦ suc. m | zero. ↦ n ]
  | zero. ↦ 𝑥 ↦ match 𝑥 [ suc. m ↦ m | zero. ↦ 0 ]]
    : N → N
  

A match on an indexed datatype, and one with a refuted (omitted) branch, are read back the same way.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Vec (A : Type) : N → Type ≔ data [ nil. : Vec A zero. | cons. : (n : N) → A → Vec A n → Vec A (suc. n) ]' -e 'axiom A : Type' -e 'axiom n : N' -e 'axiom v : Vec A n' -e 'def len (A : Type) (n : N) (v : Vec A n) : N ≔ match v [ nil. ↦ zero. | cons. k _ w ↦ suc. k ]' -e 'about (len A n v)' -e 'def Empty : Type ≔ data [ ]' -e 'def abort (X : Type) (e : Empty) : X ≔ match e [ ]' -e 'axiom e : Empty' -e 'about (abort A e)'
  match v [ cons. k 𝑥 w ↦ suc. k | nil. ↦ 0 ]
    : N
  
  match e [ ]
    : A
  

When the user supplies an explicit motive, it is stored in the match and readback applies it to each branch's indices and constructor, exactly as check_match_branches applies it to compute the type at which to check that branch.  So a dependent match displays its branches at their own specialized types.  The motive itself is read back too, at the same type of type families it was checked against, and displayed in a "return" clause.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'def T : Bool → Type ≔ [ true. ↦ N | false. ↦ Bool ]' -e 'about (let g : (b : Bool) → T b ≔ b ↦ match b return x ↦ T x [ true. ↦ zero. | false. ↦ true. ] in g)'
  b ↦ match b return x ↦ T x [ false. ↦ true. | true. ↦ 0 ]
    : (b : Bool) → T b
  

This works whatever the discriminee is, since the motive is applied to the constructor rather than substituted for a variable; here it is an axiom.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'axiom ax : Bool' -e 'def T : Bool → Type ≔ [ true. ↦ N | false. ↦ Bool ]' -e 'about (let k : T ax ≔ match ax return x ↦ T x [ true. ↦ zero. | false. ↦ true. ] in k)'
  match ax
  return x ↦ T x [ false. ↦ true. | true. ↦ 0 ]
    : T ax
  

The motive is applied to the branch's indices too, so a motive that depends on the index of an indexed datatype specializes correctly in each branch.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'def Vec (A : Type) : N → Type ≔ data [ nil. : Vec A zero. | cons. : (n : N) → A → Vec A n → Vec A (suc. n) ]' -e 'def P : N → Type ≔ [ zero. ↦ Bool | suc. _ ↦ N ]' -e 'about (let f : (n : N) (v : Vec Bool n) → P n ≔ n ↦ v ↦ match v return m _ ↦ P m [ nil. ↦ true. | cons. k x w ↦ zero. ] in f)'
  n v ↦ match v return m 𝑥 ↦ P m [ cons. k x w ↦ 0 | nil. ↦ true. ]
    : (n : N) (v : Vec Bool n) → P n
  

A stored motive is a term in the context of the match, so it is evaluated in the environment we are stuck in.  If that environment is degenerated, the result is a degeneracy of the motive: it takes a cube of that environment's dimension in each of the argument positions that typechecking gave a single element, namely the slice of the (total-dimensional) argument cube lying over the corresponding face of the match dimension.  What it computes is then a type *family* of that dimension rather than a type, so we instantiate it at the boundary of the branch body, which we get by evaluating that body in the corresponding faces of its own environment.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'axiom b0 : Bool' -e 'axiom b1 : Bool' -e 'axiom b2 : Id Bool b0 b1' -e 'def T : Bool → Type ≔ [ true. ↦ N | false. ↦ Bool ]' -e 'def g : (b : Bool) → T b ≔ b ↦ match b return x ↦ T x [ true. ↦ zero. | false. ↦ true. ]' -e 'about (refl g b2)'
  match b2 [ false. ⤇ true. | true. ⤇ refl 0 ]
    : Id T b2 (g b0) (g b1)
  

The faces of the boundary have boundaries of their own, so with a two-dimensional environment the instantiations are built up face by face.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'axiom b0 : Bool' -e 'axiom b1 : Bool' -e 'axiom b2 : Id Bool b0 b1' -e 'def T : Bool → Type ≔ [ true. ↦ N | false. ↦ Bool ]' -e 'def g : (b : Bool) → T b ≔ b ↦ match b return x ↦ T x [ true. ↦ zero. | false. ↦ true. ]' -e 'about (refl (refl g) (refl b2))'
  match refl b2 [ false. ⤇ true. | true. ⤇ 0⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ ]
    : T⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ (refl b2) (ap g (refl b0)) (ap g (refl b1)) (ap g b2) (ap g b2)
  

The stored type of a non-dependent match is instantiated the same way; it needs no arguments, being the type of every branch alike.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'axiom ax : Bool' -e 'def k : N ≔ match ax [ true. ↦ zero. | false. ↦ suc. zero. ]' -e 'about (refl k)'
  match refl ax [ false. ⤇ refl 1 | true. ⤇ refl 0 ]
    : N⁽ᵉ⁾ k k
  

Both dimensions can be positive at once: here a one-dimensional match, whose motive already takes a whole cube of arguments, is stuck in a one-dimensional environment, so each of those arguments becomes a one-dimensional cube in its turn.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def prec : N → N ≔ [ zero. ↦ zero. | suc. n ↦ n ]' -e 'axiom x : N' -e 'axiom y : N' -e 'axiom p : Id N x y' -e 'def apprec : (x y : N) → Id N x y → Id N (prec x) (prec y) ≔ x y p ↦ match p return a b c ↦ Id N (prec a) (prec b) [ zero. ⤇ zero. | suc. q ⤇ q.2 ]' -e 'about (apprec x y p)' -e 'about (refl apprec (refl x) (refl y) (refl p))'
  match p
  return a b c ↦ N⁽ᵉ⁾ (prec a) (prec b) [ suc. q ⤇ q.2 | zero. ⤇ refl 0 ]
    : N⁽ᵉ⁾ (prec x) (prec y)
  
  match refl p [ suc. q ⤇ q.22 | zero. ⤇ 0⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ ]
    : N⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ (ap prec (refl x)) (ap prec (refl y)) (apprec x y p)
        (apprec x y p)
  

The motive is not read back in a degenerated environment, so no "return" clause is displayed there: reading it back would require its type, and the type of type families that typechecking checked it against is the undegenerated one.  Nor can the motive give the type of the match *itself* there, since that would have to be instantiated at the matches at the faces of the environment, which are stuck case trees rather than values.  So when the spine is nonempty, leaving us with no other type for the match, we fall back on that spine.

  $ narya -v -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'axiom b0 : Bool' -e 'axiom b1 : Bool' -e 'axiom b2 : Id Bool b0 b1' -e 'axiom ax : N' -e 'def g : Bool → (N → N) ≔ b ↦ match b return _ ↦ N → N [ true. ↦ n ↦ n | false. ↦ n ↦ zero. ]' -e 'about (refl g b2 (refl ax))'
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant N defined
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant Bool defined
  
   ￫ info[I0001]
   ￮ axiom b0 assumed
  
   ￫ info[I0001]
   ￮ axiom b1 assumed
  
   ￫ info[I0001]
   ￮ axiom b2 assumed
  
   ￫ info[I0001]
   ￮ axiom ax assumed
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant g defined
  
   ￫ info[I0010]
   ￮ not displaying a stuck match in a degenerated environment applied to arguments; showing an application spine instead
  
  ap g b2 (refl ax)
    : N⁽ᵉ⁾ (g b0 ax) (g b1 ax)
  

A variable match refines the context instead of applying a motive, so neither the type of the match nor any motive is the type its branches were checked at.  Instead we rebind the discriminee, and any of the datatype's index variables, to the values this branch's constructor gives them -- in the environment the body is evaluated in and in the environment the type is re-evaluated in.  Refining the value along with the type is what keeps the two in step.  We try that first and read the branch back unrefined only if it fails, as typechecking tries a variable match before falling back to a non-dependent one.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'def T : Bool → Type ≔ [ true. ↦ N | false. ↦ Bool ]' -e 'about (let g : (b : Bool) → T b ≔ b ↦ match b [ true. ↦ zero. | false. ↦ true. ] in g)'
  b ↦ match b [ false. ↦ true. | true. ↦ 0 ]
    : (b : Bool) → T b
  

Rebinding the index variables is what makes a motive that depends on an index work, since the discriminee alone does not determine it.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'def Vec (A : Type) : N → Type ≔ data [ nil. : Vec A zero. | cons. : (n : N) → A → Vec A n → Vec A (suc. n) ]' -e 'def P : N → Type ≔ [ zero. ↦ Bool | suc. _ ↦ N ]' -e 'about (let f : (n : N) (v : Vec Bool n) → P n ≔ n ↦ v ↦ match v [ nil. ↦ true. | cons. k x w ↦ zero. ] in f)'
  n v ↦ match v [ cons. k x w ↦ 0 | nil. ↦ true. ]
    : (n : N) (v : Vec Bool n) → P n
  

So a branch body that mentions the discriminee, or an index variable, is displayed with the constructor substituted for it -- which is what it is in that branch, and what the body was checked against.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'def Vec (A : Type) : N → Type ≔ data [ nil. : Vec A zero. | cons. : (n : N) → A → Vec A n → Vec A (suc. n) ]' -e 'def P : N → Type ≔ [ zero. ↦ Bool | suc. _ ↦ N ]' -e 'about (let f : (n : N) (v : Vec Bool n) → P n ≔ n ↦ v ↦ match v [ nil. ↦ true. | cons. k x w ↦ n ] in f)' -e 'def U : Type ≔ data [ u. ]' -e 'def V : U → Type ≔ data [ mk. : (y : U) → V y ]' -e 'def R : U → Type ≔ [ u. ↦ V u. ]' -e 'about (let h : (x : U) → R x ≔ x ↦ match x [ u. ↦ mk. x ] in h)'
  n v ↦ match v [ cons. k x w ↦ suc. k | nil. ↦ true. ]
    : (n : N) (v : Vec Bool n) → P n
  
  x ↦ match x [ u. ↦ mk. u. ]
    : (x : U) → R x
  

Refining the *value* is what makes that sound: the body is read back from its value in the environment the match is stuck in, so refining the type, or the context, without refining that value would leave the two describing different things.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'def Vec (A : Type) : N → Type ≔ data [ nil. : Vec A zero. | cons. : (n : N) → A → Vec A n → Vec A (suc. n) ]' -e 'about (let g : (b : Bool) → Bool ≔ b ↦ match b [ true. ↦ b | false. ↦ b ] in g)' -e 'about (let h : (n : N) (v : Vec Bool n) → N ≔ n ↦ v ↦ match v [ nil. ↦ n | cons. k x w ↦ n ] in h)'
  b ↦ match b [ false. ↦ false. | true. ↦ true. ]
    : (b : Bool) → Bool
  
  n v ↦ match v [ cons. k x w ↦ suc. k | nil. ↦ 0 ]
    : (n : N) (v : Vec Bool n) → N
  

Refining outward-in is what lets a match nested inside a branch of another one be displayed: the inner match's type mentions the outer pattern variables only once the outer branch has been refined.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'def P : N → Type ≔ [ zero. ↦ Bool | suc. _ ↦ N ]' -e 'about (let q : (n : N) → P n ≔ [ zero. ↦ true. | suc. k ↦ match k [ zero. ↦ zero. | suc. j ↦ suc. j ] ] in q)'
  𝑥 ↦
  match 𝑥 [
  | suc. k ↦ match k [ suc. j ↦ suc. j | zero. ↦ 0 ]
  | zero. ↦ true.]
    : (n : N) → P n
  

A branch body that is a tuple, a comatch, or a canonical type is read back against a "self": a neutral whose value is that very struct, which supplies the field types and the components.  The enclosing neutral is that self under the branch's hypothesis, so we hand it over with this branch's value and type in place of the stuck match's.  That needs no refinement, so it works for a non-variable discriminee too.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def R : Type ≔ sig ( a : N, b : N )' -e 'axiom ax : N' -e 'about (let g : N → R ≔ [ zero. ↦ (zero., zero.) | suc. n ↦ (n, n) ] in g)' -e 'about (let h : N → R ≔ [ zero. ↦ (zero., zero.) | suc. n ↦ (n, n) ] in h ax)'
  𝑥 ↦ match 𝑥 [ suc. n ↦ (n, n) | zero. ↦ (0, 0) ]
    : N → R
  
  match ax [ suc. n ↦ (n, n) | zero. ↦ (0, 0) ]
    : R
  

The same holds for a comatch at a codatatype, for a branch body that is a canonical type declaration, and for one that is a datatype, whose constructors' output types come from the stored type family rather than from the self.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'def Stream : Type → Type ≔ A ↦ codata [ x .head : A | x .tail : Stream A ]' -e 'def z : Stream N ≔ [ .head ↦ zero. | .tail ↦ z ]' -e 'about (let s : N → Stream N ≔ [ zero. ↦ [ .head ↦ zero. | .tail ↦ z ] | suc. n ↦ [ .head ↦ n | .tail ↦ z ] ] in s)' -e 'about (let U : Bool → Type ≔ [ true. ↦ sig ( a : N ) | false. ↦ codata [ x .hd : N ] ] in U)' -e 'about (let D : Bool → Type ≔ [ true. ↦ data [ c1. (_ : N) ] | false. ↦ data [ c2. ] ] in D)'
  𝑥 ↦
  match 𝑥 [
  | suc. n ↦ [ .head ↦ n | .tail ↦ z ]
  | zero. ↦ [ .head ↦ 0 | .tail ↦ z ]]
    : N → Stream N
  
  𝑥 ↦ match 𝑥 [ false. ↦ codata [ 𝑦 .hd : N ] | true. ↦ sig ( a : N ) ]
    : Bool → Type
  
  𝑥 ↦
  match 𝑥 [
  | false. ↦ data [
    | c2. : _let.F8.0.D{…} false. ]
  | true. ↦ data [
    | c1. (𝑦 : N) : _let.F8.0.D{…} true. ]]
    : Bool → Type
  

A modal field works the same way, since keying the self by the adjunction unit acts on the value we substituted into it as well as on its spine.

  $ narya -spatial -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def C : Type ≔ codata [ (x :♭| _) .fld : N ]' -e 'def R : Type ≔ sig ( (x :♭| _) .fst : N )' -e 'axiom ax : N' -e 'about (let c : N → C ≔ [ zero. ↦ [ .fld ↦ zero. ] | suc. n ↦ [ .fld ↦ suc. zero. ] ] in c)' -e 'about (let d : N → C ≔ [ zero. ↦ [ .fld ↦ zero. ] | suc. n ↦ [ .fld ↦ suc. zero. ] ] in d ax)' -e 'about (let r : N → R ≔ [ zero. ↦ (fst ≔ zero.) | suc. n ↦ (fst ≔ suc. zero.) ] in r)'
  𝑥 ↦ match 𝑥 [ suc. n ↦ [ .fld ↦ 1 ] | zero. ↦ [ .fld ↦ 0 ] ]
    : N → C
  
  match ax [ suc. n ↦ [ .fld ↦ 1 ] | zero. ↦ [ .fld ↦ 0 ] ]
    : C
  
  𝑥 ↦ match 𝑥 [ suc. n ↦ (fst ≔ 1) | zero. ↦ (fst ≔ 0) ]
    : N → R
  

A higher field needs more: its instances are read back by degenerating the self, which reads it back and re-evaluates it, so the value we substituted in survives only if the self's spine really does evaluate to the comatch.  It does when the discriminee was refined away, since then the match reduces.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def √N : Type ≔ codata [ x .root.e : N ]' -e 'about (let t : N → √N ≔ [ zero. ↦ [ .root.e ↦ zero. ] | suc. n ↦ [ .root.e ↦ suc. zero. ] ] in t)' -e 'about (let v : N → √N ≔ [ zero. ↦ [ .root.e ↦ zero. ] | suc. n ↦ [ .root.e ↦ n.0 ] ] in v)'
  𝑥 ↦ match 𝑥 [ suc. n ↦ [ .root.e ↦ 1 ] | zero. ↦ [ .root.e ↦ 0 ] ]
    : N → √N
  
  𝑥 ↦ match 𝑥 [ suc. n ↦ [ .root.e ↦ n.0 ] | zero. ↦ [ .root.e ↦ 0 ] ]
    : N → √N
  

When it doesn't -- a higher field in a branch whose discriminee could not be refined -- projecting from the degenerated self computes nothing, and displaying it would give an instance body that is not the one the comatch stores.  We detect that and give up on the branch instead.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def √N : Type ≔ codata [ x .root.e : N ]' -e 'axiom ax : N' -e 'about (let u : N → √N ≔ [ zero. ↦ [ .root.e ↦ zero. ] | suc. n ↦ [ .root.e ↦ suc. zero. ] ] in u ax)'
  _let.F5.0.u{…} ax
    : √N
  

A higher-dimensional match reads back at its own dimension, with cube abstractions for its pattern variables.  Refining its branches means rebinding the discriminee to the whole cube of the constructor's instances, so a dependent one works too.  The dimension can come from the match itself, in which case the discriminee is an ordinary variable of a higher-dimensional type whose boundary is the separate variables that instantiate that type, and they are rebound to the constructor's corresponding faces.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'axiom b0 : Bool' -e 'axiom b1 : Bool' -e 'axiom b2 : Id Bool b0 b1' -e 'def f (x0 x1 : Bool) (x2 : Id Bool x0 x1) : N ≔ match x2 [ true. ⤇ zero. | false. ⤇ suc. zero. ]' -e 'about (f b0 b1 b2)' -e 'def U (x0 x1 : Bool) (x2 : Id Bool x0 x1) : Type ≔ match x2 [ true. ⤇ N | false. ⤇ Bool ]' -e 'about (let p : (x0 x1 : Bool) (x2 : Id Bool x0 x1) → U x0 x1 x2 ≔ x0 ↦ x1 ↦ x2 ↦ match x2 [ true. ⤇ zero. | false. ⤇ true. ] in p)'
  match b2 [ false. ⤇ 1 | true. ⤇ 0 ]
    : N
  
  x0 x1 x2 ↦ match x2 [ false. ⤇ true. | true. ⤇ 0 ]
    : (x0 : Bool) (x1 : Bool) (x2 : Bool⁽ᵉ⁾ x0 x1) → U x0 x1 x2
  

Or it can come from the environment the case tree is stuck in, as when a definition is degenerated: then the discriminee is a cube variable, and the constructor's instances sitting over the top face of the match's own dimension are its whole cube.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def pred : N → N ≔ [ zero. ↦ zero. | suc. n ↦ n ]' -e 'about (refl pred)' -e 'about (refl (refl pred))' -e 'def Vec (A : Type) : N → Type ≔ data [ nil. : Vec A zero. | cons. : (n : N) → A → Vec A n → Vec A (suc. n) ]' -e 'def len (A : Type) (n : N) (v : Vec A n) : N ≔ match v [ nil. ↦ zero. | cons. k x w ↦ suc. k ]' -e 'about (refl len)'
  𝑥 ⤇ match 𝑥.2 [ suc. n ⤇ n.2 | zero. ⤇ refl 0 ]
    : {𝑥₀ : N} {𝑥₁ : N} (𝑥₂ : N⁽ᵉ⁾ 𝑥₀ 𝑥₁) →⁽ᵉ⁾ N⁽ᵉ⁾ (pred 𝑥₀) (pred 𝑥₁)
  
  𝑥 ⤇ match 𝑥.22 [ suc. n ⤇ n.22 | zero. ⤇ 0⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ ]
    : {𝑥₀₀ : N} {𝑥₀₁ : N} {𝑥₀₂ : N⁽ᵉ⁾ 𝑥₀₀ 𝑥₀₁} {𝑥₁₀ : N} {𝑥₁₁ : N}
      {𝑥₁₂ : N⁽ᵉ⁾ 𝑥₁₀ 𝑥₁₁} {𝑥₂₀ : N⁽ᵉ⁾ 𝑥₀₀ 𝑥₁₀} {𝑥₂₁ : N⁽ᵉ⁾ 𝑥₀₁ 𝑥₁₁}
      (𝑥₂₂ : N⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ 𝑥₀₂ 𝑥₁₂ 𝑥₂₀ 𝑥₂₁)
      →⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ N⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ (ap pred 𝑥₀₂) (ap pred 𝑥₁₂) (ap pred 𝑥₂₀) (ap pred 𝑥₂₁)
  
  A n v ⤇ match v.2 [ cons. k x w ⤇ suc. k.2 | nil. ⤇ refl 0 ]
    : {A₀ : Type} {A₁ : Type} (A₂ : Type⁽ᵉ⁾ A₀ A₁) {n₀ : N} {n₁ : N}
      (n₂ : N⁽ᵉ⁾ n₀ n₁) {v₀ : Vec A₀ n₀} {v₁ : Vec A₁ n₁}
      (v₂ : Vec⁽ᵉ⁾ A₂ n₂ v₀ v₁)
      →⁽ᵉ⁾ N⁽ᵉ⁾ (len A₀ n₀ v₀) (len A₁ n₁ v₁)
  

Both at once -- a degenerated definition containing a match on a variable of higher-dimensional type -- is what the slicing is for: the variables are cubes of the environment's dimension, one over each face of the match's own.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'def f (x0 x1 : Bool) (x2 : Id Bool x0 x1) : N ≔ match x2 [ true. ⤇ zero. | false. ⤇ suc. zero. ]' -e 'about (refl f)'
  x0 x1 x2 ⤇ match x2.2 [ false. ⤇ refl 1 | true. ⤇ refl 0 ]
    : {x0₀ : Bool} {x0₁ : Bool} (x0₂ : Bool⁽ᵉ⁾ x0₀ x0₁) {x1₀ : Bool}
      {x1₁ : Bool} (x1₂ : Bool⁽ᵉ⁾ x1₀ x1₁) {x2₀ : Bool⁽ᵉ⁾ x0₀ x1₀}
      {x2₁ : Bool⁽ᵉ⁾ x0₁ x1₁} (x2₂ : Bool⁽ᵉᵉ⁾ x0₂ x1₂ x2₀ x2₁)
      →⁽ᵉ⁾ N⁽ᵉ⁾ (f x0₀ x1₀ x2₀) (f x0₁ x1₁ x2₁)
  

A match with a window modality reads back like any other: the reconstruction locks the context by the window, extends the environment through it, and rebinds the discriminee against it, exactly as checking and evaluation do.

  $ narya -spatial -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'axiom ax : Bool' -e 'def f (b :♭| Bool) : N ≔ match (b :♭| _) [ true. ↦ zero. | false. ↦ suc. zero. ]' -e 'about (f ax)' -e 'about (let p : (b :♭| Bool) → N ≔ b ↦ match (b :♭| _) [ true. ↦ zero. | false. ↦ suc. zero. ] in p)'
  match ax [ false. ↦ 1 | true. ↦ 0 ]
    : N
  
  b ↦ match b [ false. ↦ 1 | true. ↦ 0 ]
    : (b :♭| Bool) → N
  

That includes a dependent one, whose branches are refined by rebinding the modal discriminee.  A use of the discriminee is unkeyed exactly when its key is the identity two-cell on the window, which is what makes rebinding its slot rebind what that use evaluates to.

  $ narya -spatial -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'def T : Bool → Type ≔ [ true. ↦ N | false. ↦ Bool ]' -e 'about (let p : (b :♭| Bool) → T b ≔ b ↦ match (b :♭| _) [ true. ↦ zero. | false. ↦ true. ] in p)' -e 'about (let q : (b :♭| Bool) → T b ≔ b ↦ match (b :♭| _) return x ↦ T x [ true. ↦ zero. | false. ↦ true. ] in q)'
  b ↦ match b [ false. ↦ true. | true. ↦ 0 ]
    : (b :♭| Bool) → T b
  
  b ↦ match b return x ↦ T x [ false. ↦ true. | true. ↦ 0 ]
    : (b :♭| Bool) → T b
  

A match can also be stuck on an actual constructor, when that constructor's branch was refuted and we are in an inconsistent context.  A refuted branch has no body, but it does record the term of empty type that justified refuting it; and since the discriminee really is the constructor, its arguments give us the branch's environment, exactly as evaluation would build it.  So we evaluate that term there and display the refutation of it, which is what the whole match amounts to.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Empty : Type ≔ data [ ]' -e 'def D : Type ≔ data [ c0. | c1. (_ : Empty) ]' -e 'def g (x : D) : N ≔ match x [ c0. ↦ zero. ]' -e 'axiom bad : Empty' -e 'about (g (c1. bad))'
  match bad [ ]
    : N
  

The refuting variable can be modally annotated, in which case the refutation is a match through that modality as its window, just as an ordinary match on a modal discriminee is.  It has to be a modality a match could use as a window, though, since refuting a variable *is* matching against it with no branches: a variable that could not be matched on does not refute the branch either, so its constructor has to be given a clause, with a hint saying that the modality is why.

  $ narya -v -spatial -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Empty : Type ≔ data [ ]' -e 'def D : Type ≔ data [ c0. | c1. (_ :♭| Empty) ]' -e 'def g (x : D) : N ≔ match x [ c0. ↦ zero. ]' -e 'axiom bad : Empty' -e 'about (g (c1. bad))'
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant N defined
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant Empty defined
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant D defined
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant g defined
  
   ￫ info[I0001]
   ￮ axiom bad assumed
  
  match bad [ ]
    : N
  

  $ narya -v -spatial -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Empty : Type ≔ data [ ]' -e 'def D : Type ≔ data [ c0. | c1. (_ :♯| Empty) ]' -e 'def g (x : D) : N ≔ match x [ c0. ↦ zero. ]' -e 'axiom bad : Empty' -e 'about (g (c1. bad))'
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant N defined
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant Empty defined
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant D defined
  
   ￫ hint[E1102]
   ￭ command-line exec string
   1 | def g (x : D) : N ≔ match x [ c0. ↦ zero. ]
     ^ a pattern variable of empty type is annotated by modality ♯, which a match cannot use as a window, so refuting it is not allowed either
  
   ￫ error[E1300]
   ￭ command-line exec string
   1 | def g (x : D) : N ≔ match x [ c0. ↦ zero. ]
     ^ missing match clause for constructor c1
  
  [1]

Whenever a piece of a stuck case tree can't be displayed as the construct it came from, we say so as information -- visible with -v -- and show the application spine instead.


  $ narya -v -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'axiom ax : N' -e 'axiom bx : N' -e 'def plus : N → N → N ≔ [ zero. ↦ m ↦ m | suc. n ↦ m ↦ suc. (plus n m) ]' -e 'about (plus ax bx)'
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant N defined
  
   ￫ info[I0001]
   ￮ axiom ax assumed
  
   ￫ info[I0001]
   ￮ axiom bx assumed
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant plus defined
  
   ￫ info[I0010]
   ￮ not displaying an implicit stuck match applied to arguments; showing an application spine instead
  
  plus ax bx
    : N
  

  $ narya -v -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def √N : Type ≔ codata [ x .root.e : N ]' -e 'axiom ax : N' -e 'about (let u : N → √N ≔ [ zero. ↦ [ .root.e ↦ zero. ] | suc. n ↦ [ .root.e ↦ suc. zero. ] ] in u ax)'
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant N defined
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant √N defined
  
   ￫ info[I0001]
   ￮ axiom ax assumed
  
   ￫ info[I0010]
   ￮ not displaying a stuck match with a branch that reaches a higher field; showing an application spine instead
  
  _let.F5.0.u{…} ax
    : √N
  

A stuck match can also be eliminated further, when what it computes to is a function or a record.  Then the type we are handed is the type of the whole spine rather than of the match, so the match records its own type for us: an explicit motive is a type family to apply to a branch's indices and constructor, while a non-dependent match checks every branch at one type and stores that.  The match is read back at whichever it has, and the eliminations go back on the outside.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'axiom b : Bool' -e 'axiom ax : N' -e 'def g : Bool → (N → N) ≔ b ↦ match b return _ ↦ N → N [ true. ↦ n ↦ n | false. ↦ n ↦ zero. ]' -e 'about (g b ax)'
  match b return 𝑥 ↦ N → N [ false. ↦ n ↦ 0 | true. ↦ n ↦ n ] ax
    : N
  

That includes field projections, which cross to the mode the field's left adjoint comes from: the match is then displayed in our context locked by it, and the self a branch body is read back against is the neutral we were given with the spine's eliminations stripped back off, so that it too lives at the match's mode.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'def R : Type ≔ sig ( a : N, b : N )' -e 'axiom b : Bool' -e 'def g : Bool → R ≔ b ↦ match b return _ ↦ R [ true. ↦ (zero., zero.) | false. ↦ (suc. zero., zero.) ]' -e 'about (g b .a)'
  match b return 𝑥 ↦ R [ false. ↦ (1, 0) | true. ↦ (0, 0) ] .a
    : N
  

  $ narya -spatial -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'def C : Type ≔ codata [ (x :♭| _) .fld : N ]' -e 'axiom b : Bool' -e 'def g : Bool → C ≔ b ↦ match b return _ ↦ C [ true. ↦ [ .fld ↦ zero. ] | false. ↦ [ .fld ↦ suc. zero. ] ]' -e 'about ((g b :♭| _) .fld)'
  (match b
   return 𝑥 ↦ C [ false. ↦ [ .fld ↦ 1 ] | true. ↦ [ .fld ↦ 0 ] ]
   :♭| _) .fld
    : N
  

A non-dependent match needs no motive written down; here the discriminee is an axiom rather than a variable, so the match is non-dependent and its stored type is what lets the spine be put back on.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'axiom b : Bool' -e 'axiom ax : N' -e 'def k : N → N ≔ match b [ true. ↦ n ↦ n | false. ↦ n ↦ zero. ]' -e 'about (k ax)' -e 'def R : Type ≔ sig ( a : N, b : N )' -e 'def r : R ≔ match b [ true. ↦ (zero., zero.) | false. ↦ (suc. zero., zero.) ]' -e 'about (r .a)'
  match b [ false. ↦ n ↦ 0 | true. ↦ n ↦ n ] ax
    : N
  
  match b [ false. ↦ (1, 0) | true. ↦ (0, 0) ] .a
    : N
  

The reconstruction is display-only.  What remains unreadable is a dependent match with no explicit motive whose discriminee is not a variable, so that there is nothing to rebind and no motive to apply; and a *variable* match eliminated further, which records no type because it refines the context instead -- and which we may not be able to display even on its own.  In each case we fall back to displaying the application spine, as before.

  $ narya -e 'def N : Type ≔ data [ zero. | suc. (_ : N) ]' -e 'def Bool : Type ≔ data [ true. | false. ]' -e 'axiom b : Bool' -e 'def T : Bool → Type ≔ [ true. ↦ N | false. ↦ Bool ]' -e 'def f (b : Bool) : T b ≔ match b [ true. ↦ zero. | false. ↦ true. ]' -e 'about (f b)' -e 'axiom ax : N' -e 'def plus : N → N → N ≔ [ zero. ↦ m ↦ m | suc. n ↦ m ↦ suc. (plus n m) ]' -e 'axiom bx : N' -e 'about (plus ax bx)'
  f b
    : T b
  
  plus ax bx
    : N
  

The boundary of a degenerate codatatype behaves like parameters, not like the indices that the boundary of a degenerate datatype behaves like: it is the *instantiated* form that one defines elements of by comatching.  So it is that form which displays as a codatatype, showing the types of the fields such a comatch must supply -- one instance of each higher field per partial bijection.  The uninstantiated form displays as a codatatype too, in terms of a self-variable cube whose faces the instantiation replaces.

  $ narya -e 'axiom A : Type' -e 'def √A : Type ≔ codata [ x .root.e : A ]' -e 'axiom s0 : √A' -e 'axiom s1 : √A' -e 'about √A' -e 'about √A⁽ᵉ⁾' -e 'about √A⁽ᵉ⁾ s0 s1'
  codata [
  | 𝑥 .root.e : A ]
    : Type
  
  codata⁽ᵉ⁾ [
  | 𝑥 .root.e : Id A (𝑥.02 .root) (𝑥.12 .root)
  | 𝑥 .root.1 : A ]
    : Type⁽ᵉ⁾ √A √A
  
  codata⁽ᵉ⁾ [
  | 𝑥 .root.e : Id A (refl s0 .root) (refl s1 .root)
  | 𝑥 .root.1 : A ]
    : Type
  

The types displayed for the instantiated form are exactly the ones a comatch into it has to supply, as the holes show.

  $ narya -v -e 'axiom A : Type' -e 'def √A : Type ≔ codata [ x .root.e : A ]' -e 'axiom s0 : √A' -e 'axiom s1 : √A' -e 'def p : √A⁽ᵉ⁾ s0 s1 ≔ [ .root.e ↦ ? | .root.1 ↦ ? ]'
   ￫ info[I0001]
   ￮ axiom A assumed
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant √A defined
  
   ￫ info[I0001]
   ￮ axiom s0 assumed
  
   ￫ info[I0001]
   ￮ axiom s1 assumed
  
   ￫ info[I0000]
   ￮ constant p defined, containing 2 holes
  
   ￫ info[I3003]
   ￮ hole ?0:
     
     ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     Id A (refl s0 .root) (refl s1 .root)
  
   ￫ info[I3003]
   ￮ hole ?1:
     
     ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     A
  
   ￫ error[E3002]
   ￮ command-line exec string contains open holes
  
  [1]

A record type has no higher fields, so its instantiation is displayed with a self variable of the lowered dimension, letting it come out in the field-variable syntax.

  $ narya -e 'axiom A : Type' -e 'def R : Type ≔ sig ( a : A, b : A )' -e 'axiom r0 : R' -e 'axiom r1 : R' -e 'about R⁽ᵉ⁾ r0 r1'
  sig⁽ᵉ⁾ (
    a : Id A (r0 .a) (r1 .a),
    b : Id A (r0 .b) (r1 .b) )
    : Type
  
